        ANSWER:
        {'answer': 'The papers describe scalable program synthesis as relying on **modular verification / modular checking** to prune the search space.\n\nFrom the reﬁnement-type synthesis paper:\n\n- “**The key to scalable program synthesis is modular veriﬁcation.** Modularity enables the synthesizer to prune candidates for different subprograms independently, whereby combinatorially reducing the size of the search space it has to consider.”\n- It says type-directed synthesis is successful because “**a type error can be detected long before the whole program is put together**.”\n- It argues that coarse types are not enough, so it uses **reﬁnement types** to give more precise specifications and “**effectively decompos[e] into independent speciﬁcations for its components**,” which reduces the search space.\n- The method introduces “**local liquid type checking**,” “**round-trip type checking**,” and “**liquid abduction**” to support this modular decomposition.\n- It also notes that top-down propagation can filter out irrelevant partial solutions, rather than exhaustively checking complete candidates.\n\nFrom the programmatic policies paper, scalability is described differently but similarly in terms of restricting the hypothesis space:\n\n- The approach uses **state machine policies** with only a few modes, and says these are “**strongly biased towards policies that inductively generalize**.”\n- It emphasizes that compact policies are useful when “**only a few modes are required**,” because when many modes are needed “**the number of possible transition structures grows exponentially**.”\n\nSo, across the papers, scalable synthesis is described as coming from:\n1. **decomposing specifications into subgoals**,  \n2. **incremental/modular verification**, and  \n3. **restricting the search space with structure** such as types or compact policy classes.', 'usage': {'input_tokens': 6948, 'output_tokens': 367}}
        
