        ANSWER:
        {'answer': 'The papers describe scalability in program synthesis mainly as a result of **modularity** and **better representations of partial programs**.\n\nFrom the reﬁnement-type synthesis paper:\n\n- “**The key to scalable program synthesis is modular verification.** Modularity enables the synthesizer to prune candidates for different subprograms independently, whereby combinatorially reducing the size of the search space it has to consider.”\n- They argue that **type-directed approaches** help because “**a type error can be detected long before the whole program is put together**.”\n- They also say that reﬁnement types are good for scalability because they provide “**a unique combination of expressive power and decidability**,” and because a specification can often be “**effectively decomposed into independent speciﬁcations for its components**.”\n- Their method adds “**local liquid type checking**,” described as “**a new type checking mechanism for reﬁnement types**” that supports “specification decomposition,” including “**top-down propagation of over-approximate type information**” and “**modular checking of function applications**” and branching terms.\n\nFrom the neural synthesis paper:\n\n- Scalability is framed as a search problem over a “**vast, combinatorial space of possible programs**.”\n- Their technique improves scalability by giving the synthesizer a way to represent partial programs using “**blended abstract semantics**,” which combines “**concrete execution state and learned neural representations**.”\n- This helps because the system can judge whether a partial program is “**on the right track**” and “**predict where to search next**.”\n- They say this enables synthesizers to use “**more powerful language constructs, such as loops and higher-order functions**,” and to solve more programs “**for a given search budget**.”\n\nIn short, the papers present scalable synthesis as coming from:\n\n1. **Modular verification / type checking** that p